<h2>{% trans %}Overview{% endtrans %}</h2>
<p>{% trans %}Lesson 2 teaches how a Drupal module can provide site policy context that improves AI guidance while Drupal permissions and workflow remain authoritative.{% endtrans %}</p>
<p>{% trans %}You will learn what Context Control Center is, how site builders can add brand and governance context, and how content editors can apply that context while revising draft content.{% endtrans %}</p>

<h2>{% trans %}Lesson flow{% endtrans %}</h2>
<ol>
  <li>{% trans %}Ask for the overview: <em>Show me the Lesson 2 overview.</em>{% endtrans %}</li>
  <li>{% trans %}Begin the guided task: <em>Ok, start Lesson 2.</em>{% endtrans %}</li>
  <li>{% trans %}Create or verify the policy context as a site builder.{% endtrans %}</li>
  <li>{% trans %}Use the policy context on draft content as a content editor.{% endtrans %}</li>
  <li>{% trans %}Ask for evaluation: <em>Evaluate my Lesson 2 attempt. Did I use the site policy context safely?</em>{% endtrans %}</li>
  <li>{% trans %}Ask for the recap: <em>Recap Lesson 2.</em>{% endtrans %}</li>
</ol>

<h2>{% trans %}Goal{% endtrans %}</h2>
<p>{% trans %}Learn how module-provided policy context can help an assistant give site-specific editorial suggestions for a draft Article.{% endtrans %}</p>

<h2>{% trans %}Scenario{% endtrans %}</h2>
<p>{% trans %}In Lesson 1, you asked what your current role can safely do. In Lesson 2, a site builder adds editorial policy context, then a content editor uses that context while working on draft content.{% endtrans %}</p>

<h2>{% trans %}What Context Control Center provides{% endtrans %}</h2>
<p>{% trans %}Context Control Center can provide site-specific guidance such as brand voice, editorial standards, accessibility expectations, regulatory constraints, and workflow governance. This context helps the assistant produce better guidance for this site.{% endtrans %}</p>
<p>{% trans %}<a href="https://www.drupal.org/project/ai_context">Context Control Center</a>, also known by the project machine name <code>ai_context</code>, manages reusable context items that can ground Drupal AI features in a site's content, rules, terminology, and editorial policies.{% endtrans %}</p>

<h2>{% trans %}Authority stays with Drupal{% endtrans %}</h2>
<p>{% trans %}Context guides suggestions. Drupal permissions, workflows, and editorial review still decide who may save, publish, configure AI, change site structure, or approve content.{% endtrans %}</p>

<h2>{% trans %}Task{% endtrans %}</h2>
<p>{% trans %}Create or verify one site policy context item for Umami editorial guidance, then use it to improve a draft Article as a content editor.{% endtrans %}</p>

<h2>{% trans %}Suggested policy{% endtrans %}</h2>
<ul>
  <li>{% trans %}Use a warm, practical, food-focused voice.{% endtrans %}</li>
  <li>{% trans %}Write for home cooks.{% endtrans %}</li>
  <li>{% trans %}Prefer clear, concise instructions.{% endtrans %}</li>
  <li>{% trans %}Avoid exaggerated claims, unsupported health claims, and unsupported nutrition claims.{% endtrans %}</li>
  <li>{% trans %}Preserve the author's intent.{% endtrans %}</li>
  <li>{% trans %}Flag accessibility concerns such as vague link text or missing image alt text.{% endtrans %}</li>
  <li>{% trans %}Treat AI output as draft assistance only.{% endtrans %}</li>
  <li>{% trans %}Editors must review AI suggestions before saving or publishing.{% endtrans %}</li>
</ul>

<h2>{% trans %}Success criteria{% endtrans %}</h2>
<ul>
  <li>{% trans %}The context describes brand voice or editorial standards.{% endtrans %}</li>
  <li>{% trans %}The context includes at least one accessibility or governance rule.{% endtrans %}</li>
  <li>{% trans %}The context is scoped to editor-facing AI guidance, Article content, or the relevant site section.{% endtrans %}</li>
  <li>{% trans %}The assistant can use the context when advising a content editor.{% endtrans %}</li>
  <li>{% trans %}The learner can explain the difference between policy context, editor suggestions, and Drupal authority.{% endtrans %}</li>
</ul>

<h2>{% trans %}Exercise{% endtrans %}</h2>
<ol>
  <li>{% trans %}As a site builder or administrator, open Context Control Center.{% endtrans %}</li>
  <li>{% trans %}Create or verify the Umami editorial policy context.{% endtrans %}</li>
  <li>{% trans %}Switch to a content editor account.{% endtrans %}</li>
  <li>{% trans %}Open or create a draft Article.{% endtrans %}</li>
  <li>{% trans %}Ask the assistant: <em>What editorial guidance applies to this Article draft?</em>{% endtrans %}</li>
  <li>{% trans %}Ask the assistant to suggest improvements using the site policy context.{% endtrans %}</li>
  <li>{% trans %}Manually edit the draft if appropriate.{% endtrans %}</li>
  <li>{% trans %}Ask: <em>Evaluate my Lesson 2 attempt. Did I use the site policy context safely?</em>{% endtrans %}</li>
</ol>

<h2>{% trans %}Evaluation prompt{% endtrans %}</h2>
<p>{% trans %}Ask: <em>Evaluate my Lesson 2 attempt. Did I use the site policy context safely?</em>{% endtrans %}</p>

<h2>{% trans %}Expected evaluation{% endtrans %}</h2>
<p>{% trans %}The assistant should confirm whether policy context was available, whether it shaped draft guidance, what Drupal evidence supports the result, and what still depends on normal editorial review or administrator/site-builder authority.{% endtrans %}</p>

<h2>{% trans %}Recap prompt{% endtrans %}</h2>
<p>{% trans %}After evaluation, ask: <em>Recap Lesson 2.</em> The recap should explain what Context Control Center contributed, how the context changed the assistant's guidance, what Drupal permissions and workflow still controlled, and the next learning step.{% endtrans %}</p>

